ContactRoutes sets the ways into Aquemi as labelled route sheets on a drawing plate, each with its own numbered 1 → 2 → 3 strip and response clock, so a visitor on a contact page sees which route fits before the form: request a proposal, production stopped (call the service line), or visit the shop.

Origin: `inspiration/gemini/62-concept-contact-routes.png` (stacked route sheets with "Route N" tabs inside a blueprint frame, joined by a dashed route line; the urgent route a navy panel with a big call bar).

- Data: block `"type": "contact-routes"` with `routes: [{t, d, steps: [3 strings], clock, link, phone?, urgent?, current?, action?, intent?}]`. `link` is a page id. `clock` is the full response line ("Written reply within [proposal response time, working days]"); every time stays the page's own placeholder until Aquemi confirms it. `action` overrides the button label (default: the linked page's nav label); `intent` ticks options on that page's form, as on Button.
- Frame: the routes sit on a Plate (`wPlate`, class `aq-contact-routes__plate`: ruler ticks, registration crosses) on the navy, stacked full width and joined on the left by a dashed `flow` route line with an arrow into each sheet.
- Sheet: a folder tab on its top edge ("Route N" in data order, then "Urgent" with a `fail` dot and/or "This page"); a head with the title and one line on the left and the response clock and the action on the right; a step strip along the foot: numbered `flow` rings on a dashed line, arrows between, each step in mono below its ring.
- Ordinary routes are cream sheets (`limestone`, `data-theme="light"`). Current route: `current: true` (or `link` equal to the page being read) frames the sheet in `flow`, adds "This page" to the tab (`aria-current="page"`) and turns its action into a primary jump to this page's form (the form block's `nav` id; without one, the first form on the page). The other sheets link to their pages with a secondary button.
- Urgent route: `urgent: true` makes a navy panel (`data-theme="dark"`, `flow` frame with an inner hairline) with a full-width call bar (`flow-tint`, `flow` border, phone icon, "Call", the number at 32 px mono, 22 px on phones); once the number is real it becomes a `tel:` link. The urgent sheet is first in the markup, so phones show it first and it is never hidden; desktop keeps the data order through `--o`. One urgent route per block.
- Markup (`blocks/contactroutes.js`): `.aq-plate.aq-contact-routes__plate` > `ul.aq-contact-routes` of `li.aq-contact-routes__route` (`is-current`, `is-urgent`, inline `--o`, `data-theme`): `p.aq-contact-routes__tab` (`__rn`, `__mark`, `__dot`), `.aq-contact-routes__head` (`__main`: `h3.__t`, `p.__d`; `__side`: `p.__clock` with icon, `__k` "Response", `__v`; `.__action`), `p.aq-contact-routes__call` (icon, `__k`, `__num`), `ol.aq-contact-routes__steps` (`li`: `.__n` aria-hidden, `.__s`, `.__arrow` aria-hidden).
- Phones (`@container (max-width: 860px)`): a tighter plate and route line; sheets stack urgent first, keep their tabs and the horizontal 1 → 2 → 3 strip (steps 13 px sans); the head opens up so the call bar sits right under the urgent title, above the clock and the action.
- Ground: the navy page (a decision anchor), right after the hero on contact pages. Keep it to three routes and three short steps each.
- Not for a list of related pages (RelatedLinks) or for choosing a technical route by situation (Router).
